%% The ORDER is the point, and only part of it is enforced by a machine.
%% The version bump must land in the LAST commit because check-marketplace.py
%% walks marketplace.json's git history to find where each version was last set
%% and fails if that plugin's files changed after it -- so a fix made after the
%% bump costs another bump. The sabotage step is the one nothing checks for
%% you: two of crew's guard bugs passed code review and were found only by
%% running it.
%% --
%% LR, not TD: these 12 steps stacked vertically rendered 4254px tall, which
%% is not "one screen" by any definition. Condensed to 12 nodes on purpose --
%% steps that always happen together are one box, and the two boxes with a
%% heavy border are the ones that actually bite.

flowchart LR
  start(["Change under skills/ or plugin/"]) --> hook{"Registers<br/>a hook?"}
  hook -->|no| reg
  hook -->|yes| hw["Default it OFF in both menus, and add a<br/>must-block / must-allow case — then SABOTAGE it:<br/>reintroduce the bug, confirm the suite goes RED"]
  hw --> reg["Register everywhere — skills 4 places, plugins 5<br/>+ README, CHANGELOG, INSTALLATION.md"]
  reg --> tests["Local gates: pytest · pylint by EXIT CODE<br/>guard suite · sabotage all RED"]
  tests --> bump["Bump the version — in the LAST commit<br/>marketplace.json AND the plugin's own plugin.json"]
  bump --> cmp{"check-marketplace.py"}
  cmp -->|"reports drift — a fix made<br/>after the bump costs another"| bump
  cmp -->|"all checks passed"| pr["Open the PR"]
  pr --> ci["CI: marketplace · pylint · pytest-crew · mcp-servers"]
  ci --> rev{"Codex reviews<br/>ON the PR"}
  rev -->|BLOCK| tests
  rev -->|PASS| merge["gh pr merge --match-head-commit,<br/>then re-pin the README SHA"]

  note["Nothing above catches a content change with NO version<br/>bump, on a machine that already installed it: the CLI<br/>compares declared versions, reports 'already at the latest',<br/>and copies nothing. That is what the bump step is for."]
  bump -.-> note

